{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:UGB.GrobnerShirshov.CompositionLemma","@type":"LeanModule","name":"UGB.GrobnerShirshov.CompositionLemma","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:UGB.GrobnerShirshov.Basic"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:UGB.GrobnerShirshov.retraction_property","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"UGB.GrobnerShirshov.retraction_property","kind":"theorem","line":240,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/unital-groebner-bases/lean/UGB/GrobnerShirshov/CompositionLemma.lean#L240-L258","doc":"The retraction property `reduce ∘ embed ∘ reduce = reduce`\nneeded by `FormalGroups.GrobnerShirshovBasis.composition_lemma`.\n\nIf `embed` is the identity on reduced elements, then the property\nfollows from idempotency of `reduce`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:UGB.GrobnerShirshov.composition_lemma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"UGB.GrobnerShirshov.composition_lemma","kind":"theorem","line":198,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/unital-groebner-bases/lean/UGB/GrobnerShirshov/CompositionLemma.lean#L198-L236","doc":"**The Composition Lemma** (Shirshov 1962, Bokut 1976, Bergman 1978):\n\nThe reduction map is **idempotent**: `reduce (reduce f) = reduce f`.\n\nThis encodes the forward direction of the diamond lemma: a GS basis\n(all S-polynomials reduce to zero) implies that normal-form monomials\nform a `k`-basis of the quotient `k⟨G⟩ / (Σ)`.\n\nProof: `reduce f` is in normal form (by `reduce_is_normal`), so it\nreduces to itself in zero steps.  By `normal_form_unique`, the\nchosen normal form of `reduce f` must equal `reduce f`.\n\nCarries `hlc` (unit leading coefficients) since `reduce` does — see\n`Basic.lean` §6. Both hypotheses are load-bearing and for different reasons:\n`hlc` is what makes `reduce` defined at all, `hgs` is what makes its value\ncanonical. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:UGB.GrobnerShirshov.normal_form_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"UGB.GrobnerShirshov.normal_form_unique","kind":"theorem","line":166,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/unital-groebner-bases/lean/UGB/GrobnerShirshov/CompositionLemma.lean#L166-L194","doc":"**Uniqueness of normal forms**: if `f →* nf₁` and `f →* nf₂`\nwhere both `nf₁` and `nf₂` are in normal form, then `nf₁ = nf₂`.\n\nProof: by confluence, `nf₁` and `nf₂` have a common reduct `h`.\nSince both are in normal form (no reduction applies), `h = nf₁`\nand `h = nf₂`, hence `nf₁ = nf₂`.\n\nWas an axiom. Its own docstring gave the two-line proof and then listed as\nthe reason for axiomatising exactly the two things that are available:\nconfluence (§3, now a theorem) and irreducibility of normal forms\n(`MultiStepReduction.eq_of_isNormalForm`, proved in `Basic.lean` since the\nreduction relation was repaired).\n\nJustified by Bergman 1978, Theorem 1.2. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:UGB.GrobnerShirshov.confluence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"UGB.GrobnerShirshov.confluence","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/unital-groebner-bases/lean/UGB/GrobnerShirshov/CompositionLemma.lean#L139-L162","doc":"**Confluence**: any two multi-step reducts of the same element have a common\nreduct.\n\nWas an axiom. It is not one: it is `newman` applied to `local_confluence`\n(§2) and `reduction_well_founded` (§1), and those two are the only\nassumptions it ever needed. The previous docstring's reason for\naxiomatising — that an explicit derivation-height measure on\n`MultiStepReduction` was missing — is not in fact what the argument induct\non; it inducts on the accessibility of the *source*, which §1 already\nsupplies. `MultiStepReduction.cases_head` is the one piece that was missing,\nand it is three lines. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:UGB.GrobnerShirshov.newman","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"UGB.GrobnerShirshov.newman","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/unital-groebner-bases/lean/UGB/GrobnerShirshov/CompositionLemma.lean#L91-L137","doc":"**Newman's lemma**, in the form the induction actually needs: local\nconfluence plus accessibility of `f` under reduction gives confluence at `f`.\n\nThe hypothesis is taken as a plain function rather than as\n`IsGrobnerShirshovBasis`, because nothing in the argument looks at *why* the\nsystem is locally confluent — this is the abstract rewriting content, and\nkeeping it separate is what makes `confluence` below a one-line instantiation.\n\nThe proof is the standard tiling argument. Given `x →* g₁` and `x →* g₂`,\nexpose the first step of each (`MultiStepReduction.cases_head`); if either run\nis empty the other is the common reduct. Otherwise `x → a →* g₁` and\n`x → b →* g₂`, and:\n\n* local confluence at the fork `a ← x → b` gives `c` with `a →* c`, `b →* c`;\n* the induction hypothesis at `a` — legitimate because `x → a` — applied to\n  `a →* g₁` and `a →* c` gives `d` with `g₁ →* d` and `c →* d`;\n* the induction hypothesis at `b`, applied to `b →* g₂` and `b →* c →* d`,\n  gives `e` with `g₂ →* e` and `d →* e`.\n\nThen `e` is the common reduct: `g₁ →* d →* e` and `g₂ →* e`. Note that the\ntwo induction steps are *not* symmetric — the second consumes the output of\nthe first — which is why local confluence alone does not suffice and\ntermination is doing real work.\n\nReference: Newman (1942); Bergman 1978, Corollary to Theorem 1.2. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:UGB.GrobnerShirshov.local_confluence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"UGB.GrobnerShirshov.local_confluence","kind":"axiom","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/unital-groebner-bases/lean/UGB/GrobnerShirshov/CompositionLemma.lean#L62-L87","doc":"**Local confluence**: if all S-polynomials reduce to zero (i.e., the\nsystem is a GS basis), then whenever `f → g₁` and `f → g₂` by\nsingle-step reductions, there exists `h` with `g₁ →* h` and `g₂ →* h`.\n\nAxiomatised because the three-case proof (disjoint / overlap / inclusion)\nrequires `AlgElement` subtraction to form the S-polynomial `g₁ - g₂`,\nwhich is not available at the abstract level.  The argument is:\n- **Disjoint sites**: both reductions commute; apply each to the other's result.\n- **Overlap**: `g₁ - g₂` is an overlap S-polynomial, which reduces to zero\n  by `hgs.overlap_resolves`.\n- **Inclusion**: `g₁ - g₂` is an inclusion S-polynomial, which reduces to zero\n  by `hgs.inclusion_resolves`.\n\nReference: Bergman 1978, §3, proof of Theorem 1.2 (the Diamond Lemma). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:UGB.GrobnerShirshov.normal_form_exists_of_unit_lc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"UGB.GrobnerShirshov.normal_form_exists_of_unit_lc","kind":"theorem","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/unital-groebner-bases/lean/UGB/GrobnerShirshov/CompositionLemma.lean#L45-L58","doc":"Compatibility alias for the name this theorem carried while it lived here.\n\nIt is now `Basic.normal_form_exists`: once the false axiom of that name was\nremoved, the repaired statement could take the plain name, and the `_of_unit_lc`\nsuffix stopped earning its keep. Retained so the migration does not break\nreferences. ","signatureChunk":0}]}